    coverageNote: "Read from Lehninger 8 section 10.1, A printed pages 341-346 (A pdf 377-382), end to end through the chapter opening and all four subheadings (Fatty Acids Are Hydrocarbon Derivatives; Triacylglycerols Are Fatty Acid Esters of Glycerol; Triacylglycerols Provide Stored Energy and Insulation; Partial Hydrogenation of Cooking Oils Improves Their Stability but Creates Fatty Acids with Harmful Health Effects; Waxes Serve as Energy Stores and Water Repellents), plus both KEY CONVENTION boxes, TABLE 10-1 in full, FIGURES 10-1 to 10-5, and SUMMARY 10.1. THE TOC RANGE IS WRONG AT ITS START and this was measured rather than assumed: lehninger_toc.tsv gives 340-346, but A printed 340 still carries chapter 9's Data Analysis Problem on HincII, and the chapter-10 opening page (the LIPIDS title page, whose own contents block reads '10.1 Storage Lipids 341') is A printed 341. The chapter's KEY TERMS index on A printed 364 confirms it by printing 'fatty acid 341'. SUMMARY 10.1 and the opening of 10.2 share A printed 346. Content by page as read: A p.341 the chapter opening (lipids defined by insolubility in water, the four principles of cellular lipid function, the eight LIPID MAPS categories forward-referenced to Table 10-2) and the definition of a fatty acid as a carboxylic acid with a hydrocarbon chain of 4 to 36 carbons; A p.342 TABLE 10-1 in full (structure, systematic and common name, melting point, and solubility in water and in benzene at 30 degrees C, for eleven fatty acids) and the first KEY CONVENTION (the chain-length:double-bond shorthand and the delta notation); A p.343 the second KEY CONVENTION (the omega numbering and the omega-3/omega-6 families), the essential-PUFA paragraph with the dietary omega-6 to omega-3 ratios, the solubility argument, the melting-point argument and FIGURE 10-1 (packing of saturated versus cis-unsaturated chains); A p.344 free fatty acids on serum albumin, triacylglycerol structure and naming, simple versus mixed, FIGURE 10-2, and adipocytes with FIGURE 10-3; A p.345 lipases, THE TWO ADVANTAGES OF FAT OVER GLYCOGEN with their numbers, insulation in polar and hibernating animals, rancidity, and partial hydrogenation with the trans-fat health argument and FIGURE 10-4; A p.346 waxes (FIGURE 10-5, beeswax, plankton, preen glands, industrial uses) and SUMMARY 10.1. WHAT THE CZECH NODES ALREADY HAVE, so nothing here is presented as new when it is not: 8-1 has the operational definition of a lipid, the book's own admission that no exact definition is possible, the five biological functions and Tab. 8.1's three-way structural split; 8-2-1-1 has the chemical definition of a fatty acid, the three classification criteria and the whole of Tab. 8.2 with every Delta and every n-/omega- series; 8-2-1-2 has the systematic nomenclature, the two essential fatty acids, elongases and desaturases, and the QUALITATIVE rule that saturation raises the melting point while a double bond nearer the carboxyl lowers it further; 8-2-2-1 has acylglycerols, the neutral-lipid definition, the L-series statement, hydrolysis by lipases and the three biological functions of triacylglycerols; 8-2-2-2 has waxes, their component acids and alcohols, beeswax as myricyl palmitate with its printed formula, insolubility, resistance to lipases and cleavage by cerases, and the melting range 50-85 degrees C. THIS NODE DOES NOT RESTATE THOSE. It supplies the measured numbers underneath the qualitative rules, the physical mechanism (chain packing) that produces them, the energetics of fat versus glycogen, and the food-chemistry consequences. TWO PLACES WHERE LEHNINGER ARBITRATES A CZECH FLAG, both recorded in the points rather than as lehNotes because neither is a conflict the Czech node got wrong: (1) node 8-2-2-2 flags that the Czech book prints 'cetylalkohol (C11)' and suspects a typesetting error for C16 — Lehninger's independent range for wax alcohols is C16 to C30 (A p.346), which excludes C11 and includes C16, so the flag is supported from outside the book; (2) the two books give different wax melting ranges, Czech 50-85 degrees C and Lehninger 60 to 100 degrees C (A p.346), which is a difference of scope rather than a contradiction and is reported as such. LIMITS: figures were read as captions plus surrounding prose, not as images; the fatty-acid numbers in TABLE 10-1 are as printed for solubility AT 30 DEGREES C and melting point in degrees C, and no value has been converted or rounded; the chapter's eight-category LIPID MAPS table (TABLE 10-2, A p.364) is treated in L-10-4-1 where it belongs, not here.",
